INT. ABANDONED SALOON - DUSK

Dust blankets the long bar. A chandelier hangs by one chain, glass prisms scattered across the floorboards like frozen rain. Through shattered windows the sky bleeds orange into violet.

HOLLIS GREER (50s, lean as a fence post, long coat gone grey from travel) stands at the bar with a tin cup. He wipes a stool with his sleeve. Sits. Pours water from a canteen and does not drink it.

                    HOLLIS
          (to the empty room)
          Evening, folks.

Nothing. A loose shutter bangs.

                    HOLLIS (CONT'D)
          Place looks like it's seen better days. Same as me.

He sets the cup down. Looks at the mirror behind the bar. A crack runs through it like a river on a map. His own reflection splits in two.

                    HOLLIS (CONT'D)
          (muttering)
          Don't you start.

A voice rings from outside, clear in the thin air.

                    DORA (O.S.)
          Hollis Greer!

He doesn't flinch. He sets down the cup, slow.

                    HOLLIS
          I hear you.

                    DORA (O.S.)
          Thought you might. Come out.

                    HOLLIS
          I'm tired, Dora.

                    DORA (O.S.)
          So am I. I rode three hundred miles. Come out or I come in.

He rubs his face. Stands. Settles his coat. Moves to the doorway but does not cross it.

                    HOLLIS
          Everybody who's listening, I'd like it noted I asked nicely.

                    DORA (O.S.)
          Nobody's listening.

                    HOLLIS
          The horses are.

The batwing doors creak. Spurs ring.

THEODORA "DORA" SIX-CROWS (40s, braided hair silver at the roots, a Winchester slung across her back, face weathered like bark) pushes through. Her eyes take in the wreck of the saloon, the chandelier, the cracked mirror, the man.

                    DORA
          You look old.

                    HOLLIS
          You look exactly the same.

                    DORA
          That's not a compliment.

                    HOLLIS
          It wasn't meant as one.

She walks to the bar. Takes his cup. Drinks. Sets it back.

                    DORA
          You still owe me a horse.

                    HOLLIS
          I owe you more than a horse.

                    DORA
          Start with the horse.
